Annotations: |
  This cell is the app's core recipient domain model: every conversation, message, call, and
  contact ultimately resolves to a `Recipient` identified by a `RecipientId`. `RecipientId` wraps
  an opaque database row id and is never constructed directly by application code — it is always
  obtained through one of the module's own resolution/lookup entry points. `Recipient` instances
  may be partially resolved (defaults filled in while the real record loads from disk in the
  background) or fully resolved; callers needing a live-updating handle use `LiveRecipient`,
  vended by `LiveRecipientCache`.

---

"RecipientId(id: Long)":
  location: RecipientId.kt
  annotations: |
    Opaque, serializable identifier for a recipient (a person, group, or distribution list).

    `id`: the underlying database row id; a raw value of 0 is invalid.

    The primary constructor is private — instances are obtained through the type's own resolution
    entry points, not built directly: from a raw id, from a service-id/e164 pair (looking up or
    inserting the corresponding row), or from a group id. UNKNOWN is a reserved sentinel value
    used before a recipient has been resolved.
  properties:
    "isUnknown -> Boolean": |
      True if this id is the reserved UNKNOWN sentinel rather than a real row id.
  methods:
    "toLong() -> value:Long": |
      The underlying raw database row id.
    "toQueueKey(forMedia: Boolean) -> key:String": |
      A stable string key for scoping a job-queue or executor lane to this recipient.

      `forMedia`: when true, appends a media-specific suffix so media work for this recipient
      serializes on its own lane, independent of non-media work.
    "serialize() -> value:String": |
      String form of the id, used for persistence and equality checks across process boundaries.

"Recipient(id: RecipientId)":
  location: Recipient.kt
  annotations: |
    A recipient: a person, a group, "note to self", the release-notes recipient, or a
    distribution list (story audience). May be partially resolved — reading most properties on an
    unresolved instance returns a placeholder pending a background load from disk.

    `id`: the identifier this instance represents.

    Obtained via resolved, self, live, resolvedList, or one of the external* factory
    methods below — never constructed directly by callers.
  properties:
    "isSelf -> Boolean": |
      True if this recipient represents the local user's own account.
    "isBlocked -> Boolean": |
      True if the local user has blocked this recipient.
    "isGroup -> Boolean": |
      True if this recipient represents a V1 or V2 group rather than an individual or
      distribution list.
    "isRegistered -> Boolean": |
      True if this recipient is a known, currently-registered Signal user (as opposed to an SMS
      contact or a Signal user who has since deregistered).
    "hasServiceId -> Boolean": |
      True if a service identifier (ACI/PNI) is known for this recipient, as opposed to only an
      e164 or group id.
    "isReleaseNotes -> Boolean": |
      True if this is the special release-notes recipient.
  methods:
    "getDisplayName(context: Context) -> name:String": |
      The name to render for this recipient in UI: profile name, system contact name, group
      title, or a placeholder for an unresolved/unknown recipient, in that order of precedence.

      `context`: used to resolve fallback/placeholder string resources.
    "live() -> handle:LiveRecipient": |
      A live-updating handle for this recipient's id, backed by `LiveRecipientCache`. Use this
      when a caller needs to observe future changes rather than read a single snapshot.
    "resolved(id: RecipientId) -> recipient:Recipient": |
      Fully resolves the recipient for `id`, reading from disk/cache as needed. May block; not
      for use on paths that must stay off the calling thread's slow path.
    "resolvedList(ids: List<RecipientId>) -> recipients:List<Recipient>": |
      Fully resolves each id in `ids`, in the same order.

      `ids`: identifiers to resolve.
    "self() -> recipient:Recipient": |
      The local user's own, fully-resolved recipient.

"RecipientRepository()":
  location: RecipientRepository.kt
  annotations: |
    A place for recipient-related operations that are otherwise duplicated across many call
    sites: primarily, checking whether a phone number or a set of `RecipientId`s correspond to
    registered Signal users, consulting local data first and only falling back to a directory
    lookup over the network when necessary.
  methods:
    "lookup(phone: PhoneNumber) -> result:PhoneLookupResult": |
      Determines whether `phone` belongs to a registered Signal user.

      `phone`: the module's own validated phone-number value wrapper.
      `result`: one of found (with the resolved `Recipient`), not-found, invalid-phone, or
      network-error.

      Algorithm:
      1. Check the local recipient store for an existing, discoverable, registered match.
      2. If none is found locally, perform a directory lookup for the number.
      3. Map the outcome onto `PhoneLookupResult`.
    "lookup(recipientIds: List<RecipientId>) -> result:IdLookupResult": |
      Determines which of `recipientIds` are currently registered Signal users, refreshing
      registration status over the network for any that appear stale or unregistered before
      answering.

      `recipientIds`: identifiers to check.
      `result`: found-all, or found-some paired with the unregistered subset.

"LiveRecipient()":
  location: LiveRecipient.java
  annotations: |
    A live-updating, RxJava-backed handle to a single recipient's latest known state. Never
    constructed directly by callers — obtained via Recipient.live() or `LiveRecipientCache`.
  methods:
    "getId() -> id:RecipientId": |
      The identifier this handle tracks; stable even as the underlying `Recipient` snapshot it
      returns from get() changes.
    "get() -> recipient:Recipient": |
      The most recently known snapshot, which may or may not be fully resolved yet.
    "observable() -> stream:Observable<Recipient>": |
      An RxJava stream that emits a new snapshot each time this recipient's state changes.
    "resolve() -> recipient:Recipient": |
      Ensures a fully-resolved snapshot is loaded (reading from disk if the current snapshot is
      still resolving) and returns it. May block the calling thread.
    "refresh(id: RecipientId)": |
      Forces a reload of the underlying recipient from disk, even if a resolved snapshot is
      already cached, and notifies observers.

      `id`: expected to match getId(); a mismatch is logged but does not prevent the refresh.

"LiveRecipientCache(context: Context)":
  location: LiveRecipientCache.java
  annotations: |
    Process-wide, size-bounded (LRU) cache of `LiveRecipient` handles keyed by `RecipientId`,
    plus the resolution of "who is the local user" (self). Owns the background executor that
    resolves newly-created, not-yet-resolved handles.

    `context`: application context used for downstream resolution work.
  methods:
    "remap(oldId: RecipientId, newId: RecipientId)": |
      Repoints a cache entry from `oldId` to `newId` after two recipient rows have been merged
      into one.
    "refresh(recipientIds: Collection<RecipientId>)": |
      Re-reads and updates the cached snapshot for each id in `recipientIds` that is already
      present in the cache; ids not already cached are left untouched.
    "addToCache(newRecipients: Collection<Recipient>)": |
      Inserts or updates cache entries for `newRecipients`. An entry already present is updated
      only if the incoming snapshot is more resolved than what is cached, or vice versa
      triggers a background resolve.
    "warmUp()": |
      Eagerly resolves and caches a bounded set of recently-active thread recipients and contacts
      on a background executor, to reduce cold-start cache misses. Idempotent — a second call is
      a no-op.
    "clearSelf()": |
      Forgets the cached "local user" recipient id, forcing it to be re-derived on next access.
    "clear()": |
      Drops every cached entry.

